genesis: causal inference in observational studies — 7 classes, 13 instances, 2 calculator stubs

Domain: how do we identify causal effects from data not generated by a randomized
experiment?

Class schema (BFO-aligned, single-elector governance):
  confounding (disposition) — variables that induce non-causal treatment-outcome associations
  identification (quality) — conditions under which a causal estimand is recoverable
  intervention (process) — externally setting a variable's value; the do-operator
  estimand (quality) — a precisely stated causal quantity; the target of identification
  assumption (quality) — unverifiable conditions required for identification to hold
  estimator (disposition) — statistical procedures that recover estimands from data
  study-design (process) — data-collection structures that constrain which assumptions hold

Seed instances (13):
  intervention: binary-treatment-intervention
  estimand: average-treatment-effect (ATE), local-average-treatment-effect (LATE)
  assumption: conditional-ignorability, sutva, exclusion-restriction
  confounding: unmeasured-confounder, selection-bias
  study-design: natural-experiment
  identification: backdoor-identification, instrumental-variable-identification
  estimator: inverse-probability-weighting (IPW), two-stage-least-squares (2SLS)

Key relationships established:
  backdoor-identification requires conditional-ignorability and sutva, targets ATE
  instrumental-variable-identification requires exclusion-restriction and sutva, targets LATE
  natural-experiment enables IV identification, rules out selection-bias, supports exclusion-restriction
  IPW recovers ATE and operationalizes backdoor-identification
  2SLS recovers LATE and operationalizes IV identification
  ATE decomposes-into LATE (complier subpopulation)
  unmeasured-confounder threatens backdoor-identification

